    'article' => [
        'intro' => <<<'TXT'
        Egg Incubation Hatch Date works out Incubation period and Hatch - day of the year from Species, Day of month set and Month set. The arithmetic is the whole method: incubation period is worked out from species by one formula, applied once. Incubation period comes back in days unless you switch the unit chip beside the box.
        TXT,
        'sections' => [
            ['title' => 'How the Egg Incubation Hatch Date calculator works', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>There is one relationship behind Egg Incubation Hatch Date: it takes 3 numbers and reports Incubation period, Hatch - day of the year and Hatch month.</p><p>The relationship it solves reads Incubation period = Species.</p><p>The answers are Incubation period, Hatch - day of the year, Hatch month and Hatch day of month; they are reported together because a single calculation usually settles more than one question.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What to enter, and what comes back', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Fill in 3 boxes and the page has everything it needs.</p><ul><li><strong>Species</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Day of month set</strong> — in day. 1-31</li><li><strong>Month set</strong> — in month. 1-12</li></ul><p>The answers come back as Incubation period in days, Hatch - day of the year in day, Hatch month in month, Hatch day of month in day, Candle on day in days, Lockdown month in month, Lockdown day of month in day. Those boxes are written by the page, not by you, so there is nothing to submit.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'A worked example with numbers from this page', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Take the values the form starts on, Species = 21, Day of month set = 10 day and Month set = 3 month; the answers are <strong>Incubation period</strong> = 21 days, <strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> = 88 day, <strong>Hatch month</strong> = 3 month, <strong>Hatch day of month</strong> = 29 day, <strong>Candle on day</strong> = 7 days, <strong>Lockdown month</strong> = 3 month and <strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> = 26 day.</p><ul><li><strong>Incubation period</strong> = 21 days</li><li><strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> = 88 day</li><li><strong>Hatch month</strong> = 3 month</li><li><strong>Hatch day of month</strong> = 29 day</li><li><strong>Candle on day</strong> = 7 days</li><li><strong>Lockdown month</strong> = 3 month</li><li><strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> = 26 day</li></ul><p>Spelled out with this form's labels, the arithmetic is <strong>Incubation period</strong> = <code>Species</code>.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Reading the answer', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>A sanity check costs one glance: the page&#039;s defaults give <strong>Incubation period</strong> = 21, <strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> = 88, <strong>Hatch month</strong> = 3, <strong>Hatch day of month</strong> = 29, <strong>Candle on day</strong> = 7, <strong>Lockdown month</strong> = 3 and <strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> = 26. An answer far from that is more often a unit entered on the wrong row than a formula that has stopped working.</p><p>As far as Incubation period is concerned the numbers are unitless — it neither converts nor guesses a unit, so enter every value in the same scale you intend to read the answer in.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'The formula, and what turns it round', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The relationship is shown here as the page solves it, with the form&#039;s labels standing where the variable names would.</p><ul><li><strong>Incubation period</strong> = <code>Species</code></li><li><strong>Candle on day</strong> = <code>round(Species × 0.33,0)</code></li></ul><p>Each line below is the same relationship arranged for a different unknown. That is what makes the page work in both directions, and it is worth knowing which line the page used for your answer:</p><ul><li><strong>Incubation period</strong> = <code>Species</code> — the form to use when Species are the values you have.</li><li><strong>Candle on day</strong> = <code>round(Species × 0.33,0)</code> — the form to use when Species are the values you have.</li></ul><p>Because the equation is written once per variable, the page works in either direction: any one of Incubation period, Hatch - day of the year and Hatch month can be the unknown.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Checking an answer by hand', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Change one box only — Species, from 21 to 42 — and the answers go from <strong>Incubation period</strong> 21 days, <strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> 88 day, <strong>Hatch month</strong> 3 month, <strong>Hatch day of month</strong> 29 day, <strong>Candle on day</strong> 7 days, <strong>Lockdown month</strong> 3 month and <strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> 26 day to <strong>Incubation period</strong> 42 days, <strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> 109 day, <strong>Hatch month</strong> 4 month, <strong>Hatch day of month</strong> 19 day, <strong>Candle on day</strong> 14 days, <strong>Lockdown month</strong> 4 month and <strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> 16 day.</p><p>That is the check worth doing before a result is trusted: move each input by a factor you can hold in your head and see that the answer moves the way the formula says it should.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where the numbers usually go wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>The answer typed back in.</strong> Incubation period, Hatch - day of the year and Hatch month are results, and each can be typed into. Putting a past answer back into one of them turns the calculation round and solves for whichever box is then empty — useful, but only if that is what you meant to do.</li><li><strong>Species left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into Species is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>Day of month set left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into Day of month set is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>Month set left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into Month set is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>Measurements mixed across scales.</strong> Two numbers measured in different units, or one taken at the start of a season and one at the end, are the commonest source of an answer that is internally consistent and still wrong. Convert Species to one scale before entering anything.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each box means', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Before the arithmetic, the form itself. Each box below is named the way it is named on the page, with the unit it opens on and the error it invites if it is filled in carelessly.</p><ul><li><strong>Species</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Incubation period, Hatch - day of the year and Hatch month. It is picked from a list, and the list matters — the options are not labels for the same number. Have it to hand before you start: Species is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Day of month set</strong> is measured in day. The page reads it when it works out Hatch - day of the year, Hatch month and Hatch day of month. Have it to hand before you start: Day of month set is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>Month set</strong> is measured in month. The page reads it when it works out Hatch - day of the year, Hatch month and Hatch day of month. Have it to hand before you start: Month set is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li></ul><p>None of the boxes has to be filled in a particular order. The page reads them together and works out which single value is missing, so the last one you fill is the one it answers from.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each result tells you', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The panel reports 7 values. Here is what each one is for, and what to compare it against.</p><ul><li><strong>Incubation period</strong> is a plain number and the page's own defaults put it at 21. It is worked out from Species. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li><li><strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> is a plain number and the page's own defaults put it at 88. It is worked out from Month set, Day of month set and Species. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li><li><strong>Hatch month</strong> is a plain number and the page's own defaults put it at 3. It is worked out from Month set, Day of month set and Species. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li><li><strong>Hatch day of month</strong> is a plain number and the page's own defaults put it at 29. It is worked out from Month set, Day of month set and Species. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li><li><strong>Candle on day</strong> is a plain number and the page's own defaults put it at 7. It is worked out from Species. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li><li><strong>Lockdown month</strong> is a plain number and the page's own defaults put it at 3. It is worked out from Month set, Day of month set and Species. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li><li><strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> is a plain number and the page's own defaults put it at 26. It is worked out from Month set, Day of month set and Species. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. Nothing about it is saved, so if the figure matters, copy it out — the page will not remember it for you.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'How much each input moves the answer', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>A percentage change tells you where the answer is most fragile. Take the defaults this page opens with, raise one box by ten per cent, and re-solve:</p><table class="calc-table"><thead><tr><th>Box</th><th>Incubation period</th><th>Hatch - day of the year</th><th>Hatch month</th><th>Hatch day of month</th><th>Candle on day</th><th>Lockdown month</th><th>Lockdown day of month</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Species</strong> +10%</td><td>+10%</td><td>+2.39%</td><td>+33.33%</td><td>-99.66%</td><td>+14.29%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+8.08%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Day of month set</strong> +10%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+1.14%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+3.45%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+3.85%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Month set</strong> +10%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+35.23%</td><td>+33.33%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+0%</td><td>+33.33%</td><td>+0%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The steepest column belongs to Species: ten per cent there moves an answer by about 99.7 per cent, which is why that is the number to measure twice. Every box on the form carries some of the answer; none of them can be estimated without moving the result.</p><p>This is the same arithmetic a spreadsheet model would do, done here with the page&#039;s own formulas. It is worth repeating at your own numbers rather than the defaults: sensitivity is a property of the formula, but the size of the effect depends where on the curve you are.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Two more scenarios, worked through', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>One example proves the page computes. Two more show what it does when the inputs change, which is what a reader usually wants to know before trusting it:</p><ul><li>With Species = 10.5, Day of month set = 5 day and Month set = 1.5 month, the answer is <strong>Incubation period</strong> = 10.5 days, <strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> = 44.5 day, <strong>Hatch month</strong> = 2 month, <strong>Hatch day of month</strong> = 13.5 day, <strong>Candle on day</strong> = 3 days, <strong>Lockdown month</strong> = 2 month and <strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> = 10.5 day.</li><li>With Species = 63, Day of month set = 10 day and Month set = 3 month, the answer is <strong>Incubation period</strong> = 63 days, <strong>Hatch - day of the year</strong> = 130 day, <strong>Hatch month</strong> = 5 month, <strong>Hatch day of month</strong> = 10 day, <strong>Candle on day</strong> = 21 days, <strong>Lockdown month</strong> = 5 month and <strong>Lockdown day of month</strong> = 7 day.</li></ul><p>Read the three runs side by side and the shape of the relationship is visible without reading the formula: halving every input does not halve the answer, because the inputs do not all sit in the numerator.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Limits worth knowing', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>It scales in a straight line.</strong> The relationship behind Incubation period is a straight-line one: proportionally more input gives proportionally more output, which is what makes the sensitivity table above a set of constants rather than a curve.</li><li><strong>It is a formula, not a model of your case.</strong> Nothing here corrects for measurement error, correlation between the inputs, or conditions outside the range the relationship was written for. Those are assumptions a reader brings; the arithmetic simply does what it says.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where this calculation comes up', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Reach for this page when Species, Day of month set and Month set is what you already know and Incubation period and Hatch - day of the year is what you need — the reverse of that situation is what the rearranged form is for.</p><p>It is worth having the calculation written down even when you only need the answer once: an entered figure can be checked, and a remembered one cannot.</p><p>If the answer you need is a step further on, the nearest pages are <a href="/biology/stems-per-hectare">Stems Per Hectare Calculator</a>, <a href="/biology/stomatal-density">Stomatal Density Calculator</a> and <a href="/biology/sulfur-soil-acidifier">Sulfur Soil Acidifier Calculator</a>.</p>
